   Port 5432 is PostgreSQL's default port. When your machine boots, Anaconda's bundled PostgreSQL starts automatically as
   a Windows service and binds to 0.0.0.0:5432 — meaning it claims that port on every network interface. It does this
  before Docker even starts.

  When you run docker compose up, Docker tries to forward host port 5432 into the container's port 5432. But Windows'
  networking stack allows this to appear to succeed (both processes show up in netstat), while in practice the local
  Anaconda postgres wins the race and gets actual TCP connections.

  What happens when asyncpg connects

  When alembic runs and asyncpg opens a connection to 127.0.0.1:5432, the OS routes that TCP connection to the Anaconda
  PostgreSQL server — not the Docker container. That server has no knowledge of the app user or the pharmdb database. It
   only knows about whatever was set up locally in Anaconda. So it rejects the credentials with password authentication
  failed.

  The Docker container was healthy and running fine — it just wasn't reachable on port 5432 from the host.

  Why netstat showed two listeners

  Docker Desktop on Windows uses a network proxy (the com.docker.backend process) to handle port forwarding between the
  host and containers. That proxy also binds to 5432, which is why both PIDs showed up. But the Anaconda postgres
  process got there first and handled the actual incoming connections.

  Why the fix works

  Changing Docker's mapping to 5433:5432 means: "forward host port 5433 to container port 5432." Port 5433 is free —
  Anaconda's postgres isn't listening there — so Docker's proxy owns it unambiguously. Connections to 127.0.0.1:5433 go
  straight to your Docker container with the right credentials, and everything works.